{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795491115429,"sessionID":"ses_df463e6c2ffeJIQ0xCaosxh4r8","part":{"partID":"prt_20b9c3079001YYTMSztDURZQRX","sessionID":"ses_df463e6c2ffeJIQ0xCaosxh4r8","messageID":"msg_20b9c2b3d0017Mqm33UaVU6z2x","type":"tool","id":"call_96df8f520c7149509ada0eac","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/workspace/in/packet.md"},"output":"Read file /workspace/in/packet.md, lines 1-7\n1: # Advisory critic packet — round 1\n2: \n3: Model: new_keynesian · variant: R1 · card hash: `bdaebdd440873f3ad16471fe24f48702db886d74b7e5eebb5db699c41729c581`\n4: \n5: The mechanical gate is authoritative. Your role is advisory: return typed defects and a recommendation, but do not claim to grant PASS.\n6: \n7: Files in /workspace/in: spec.yaml, defects.json, diagnostics.json, metrics_gate.json (pass/fail only), src/ (code snapshot).","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /workspace/in/packet.md, lines 1-7\n1: # Advisory critic packet — round 1\n2: \n3: Model: new_keynesian · variant: R1 · card hash: `bdaebdd440873f3ad16471fe24f48702db886d74b7e5eebb5db699c41729c581`\n4: \n5: The mechanical gate is authoritative. Your role is advisory: return typed defects and a recommendation, but do not claim to grant PASS.\n6: \n7: Files in /workspace/in: spec.yaml, defects.json, diagnostics.json, metrics_gate.json (pass/fail only), src/ (code snapshot)."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/workspace/in/packet.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1795491115129,"end":1795491115429}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795491115437,"sessionID":"ses_df463e6c2ffeJIQ0xCaosxh4r8","part":{"partID":"prt_20b9c309b001F1QYoThew0tIW3","sessionID":"ses_df463e6c2ffeJIQ0xCaosxh4r8","messageID":"msg_20b9c2b3d0017Mqm33UaVU6z2x","type":"tool","id":"call_1bfff8e1d72c4d18a25b83f6","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/workspace/in/spec.yaml"},"output":"Read file /workspace/in/spec.yaml, lines 1-238\n1: school: new_keynesian\n2: model_id: new_keynesian\n3: version: 1.0.1\n4: status: frozen\n5: frozen_at: \"2026-11-23\"\n6: primary_target: ipca_12m\n7: validation_end: \"2019-12-31\"\n8: description: >\n9:   Monthly semi-structural New Keynesian QPM for Brazil. A hybrid NK Phillips\n10:   curve with survey expectations and an output gap, an IS curve driven by the\n11:   ex-ante real Selic rate, and a Taylor rule with an imposed neutral real rate.\n12:   GDP is a satellite bridge from IBC-Br; it is shared with the IBC-Br benchmark\n13:   and is not evidence for the New Keynesian block.\n14: \n15: changelog:\n16:   - version: 1.0.0\n17:     note: Initial pre-registration.\n18:   - version: 1.0.1\n19:     note: >\n20:       Data-contract and gate corrections before the second loop run. The\n21:       estimation start is now explicitly 2005-01 (the code previously began in\n22:       2003 because of the IBC-Br availability). The COVID dummy is used in\n23:       forecasts only when the origin is already inside the COVID window. The\n24:       gap description now states that the HP filter is two-sided within the\n25:       estimation sample, re-estimated at each origin, with no post-origin data.\n26:       GDP is marked as a shared satellite, not a school-specific forecast.\n27:       Refutation tests are machine-readable. Validation targets are purged at\n28:       the validation end so that no 2020 outcome enters model selection.\n29: \n30: equations:\n31:   is: >\n32:     gap_t = a0 + a1*gap_{t-1} + a2*gap_{t-2} + a3*(r_{t-1} - r*) + e_t,\n33:     where r_{t-1} = selic_{t-1} - Focus12m_{t-1} and r* = 4.0 is calibrated.\n34:   nkpc: >\n35:     pi_t = c + b1*pi_{t-1} + b2*pi_{t-2} + (1 - b1 - b2)*pi_e_t\n36:            + b4*gap_{t-1} + b5*dfx_{t-1} + e_t,\n37:     with long-run neutrality imposed (b1 + b2 + b3 = 1).\n38:   taylor: >\n39:     i_t - target_t - r* = rho*(i_{t-1} - target_t - r*)\n40:       + g1*(pi12_{t-1} - target_t) + g2*gap_{t-1} + e_t.\n41:     R3 replaces pi12_{t-1} with the Focus 12m expectation at t.\n42:   gdp_bridge: >\n43:     gdp_qq_t = c0 + c1*ibc_qq_t + c2*ibc_qq_{t-1} + e_t.\n44:     R1 constructs the IBC-Br path from trend growth plus gap changes.\n45:     R2/R3 construct it from an AR(1) on monthly IBC-Br growth. The R2/R3 path\n46:     is identical to the IBC-Br benchmark by construction; this is a satellite,\n47:     not evidence for the New Keynesian block.\n48:   expectations: >\n49:     pi_e_t is the monthly equivalent of the Focus 12-month-ahead median:\n50:     ((1 + focus12/100)^(1/12) - 1)*100. Survey expectations, not\n51:     model-consistent expectations.\n52:   gap: >\n53:     gap_t = 100*(log(IBC-Br_t) - HP_trend(log(IBC-Br_t))) with lambda = 129600.\n54:     The filter is two-sided within the estimation sample and is re-estimated at\n55:     each origin on data through the origin. No observation after the origin is\n56:     used. Endpoint estimates are biased and subject to revision.\n57: \n58: observables:\n59:   pi:\n60:     series: ipca_m\n61:     freq: M\n62:     transform: none\n63:     release_lag_days: 10\n64:   activity:\n65:     series: ibc_br\n66:     freq: M\n67:     transform: log, HP cycle\n68:     release_lag_days: 20\n69:   policy_rate:\n70:     series: selic_target\n71:     freq: D\n72:     transform: month-end last\n73:     release_lag_days: 0\n74:   expectations:\n75:     series: focus_ipca_12m\n76:     freq: D\n77:     transform: month-end last, monthly equivalent\n78:     release_lag_days: 0\n79:   fx:\n80:     series: fx_usdbrl\n81:     freq: M\n82:     transform: 100*dlog\n83:     release_lag_days: 1\n84:   gdp:\n85:     series: gdp_qq\n86:     freq: Q\n87:     transform: none\n88:     release_lag_days: 60\n89:     role: satellite\n90:     shared_with: ibc_bridge\n91:   inflation_target:\n92:     series: data/reference/inflation_targets.csv\n93:     freq: annual\n94:     transform: year-end target\n95: \n96: identification: >\n97:   IS curve: OLS with Newey-West (12 lags). The real-rate coefficient is\n98:   identified by variation in the ex-ante real Selic rate; r* is calibrated, not\n99:   estimated. NKPC: OLS with HAC; homogeneity is imposed, so the expectations\n100:   coefficient is 1 - b1 - b2 rather than freely estimated. Taylor rule: OLS\n101:   with HAC and the neutral real rate imposed, so the constant is not free.\n102:   The output gap is a filtered observable, not a latent state estimated by\n103:   Kalman filter. The model is estimated equation-by-equation; no system\n104:   estimator, no GMM, no IV. This is a forecasting QPM, not a full DSGE.\n105:   Simultaneity between the policy rate and inflation is a known limitation.\n106: \n107: calibration:\n108:   rstar: 4.0\n109:   hp_lambda: 129600\n110:   estimation_start: \"2005-01-01\"\n111:   covid_dummy: \"2020-03..2020-12; in estimation when identified; in forecasts only when the origin is inside the window\"\n112:   inflation_target_source: \"CMN annual targets; continuous 3.0% target from 2025\"\n113: \n114: estimation:\n115:   method: OLS with Newey-West HAC standard errors (12 lags)\n116:   frequency: monthly\n117:   start: \"2005-01\"\n118:   sample: expanding window\n119:   software: statsmodels 0.15\n120: \n121: forecast_recipe: >\n122:   At origin t (last business day of month M): IPCA and IBC-Br are available\n123:   through M-1; the Selic target and Focus 12m are available through t. The\n124:   Focus 12m expectation is held at its origin value in monthly-equivalent\n125:   terms. FX is a random walk after the first forecast month (dfx = 0). The\n126:   COVID dummy is set to 1 for forecast months inside 2020-03..2020-12 only when\n127:   the origin itself is inside that window; a pre-COVID origin cannot know the\n128:   regime, so the dummy is 0 for its forecast months. The policy rate path is\n129:   iterated from the Taylor rule; the gap and inflation paths are iterated from\n130:   the IS and NKPC. GDP is mapped by the shared satellite bridge. The 12-month\n131:   IPCA rate is derived by compounding the monthly path; it is never forecast\n132:   directly.\n133: \n134: refutation_conditions:\n135:   - A negative or statistically insignificant output-gap coefficient in the NKPC across sub-samples would refute the model's core transmission channel.\n136:   - A non-positive FX pass-through coefficient would refute the declared external channel.\n137:   - A non-negative real-rate coefficient in the IS curve would refute the demand channel.\n138:   - A non-positive inflation response in the Taylor rule, or a smoothing coefficient outside (0,1), would refute the policy block.\n139:   - Failure of Mincer-Zarnowitz calibration at the 12-month inflation horizon after the revision ladder would refute the model's forecasting claim.\n140:   - Failure to add value over the Focus consensus at the core horizons would refute the claim that the structural block is useful beyond the survey anchor.\n141: \n142: refutation_tests:\n143:   - id: nkpc_gap_significance\n144:     block: nkpc\n145:     key: gap_1\n146:     stat: tstat\n147:     op: abs_ge\n148:     threshold: 1.65\n149:     code: D1\n150:     severity: high\n151:     message: \"Output-gap coefficient in the NKPC is statistically insignificant; the card's core transmission refutation condition is triggered.\"\n152:   - id: fx_pass_through_sign\n153:     block: nkpc\n154:     key: dfx_1\n155:     stat: coef\n156:     op: gt\n157:     threshold: 0.0\n158:     code: D7\n159:     severity: high\n160:     message: \"FX pass-through coefficient is non-positive; the external channel violates the declared sign restriction.\"\n161:   - id: is_real_rate_sign\n162:     block: is\n163:     key: rr\n164:     stat: coef\n165:     op: lt\n166:     threshold: 0.0\n167:     code: D7\n168:     severity: high\n169:     message: \"Real-rate coefficient in the IS curve is non-negative; the demand channel is refuted.\"\n170:   - id: taylor_inflation_sign\n171:     block: taylor\n172:     key: pi_gap\n173:     stat: coef\n174:     op: gt\n175:     threshold: 0.0\n176:     code: D7\n177:     severity: high\n178:     message: \"Taylor-rule inflation response is non-positive; the policy block is refuted.\"\n179: \n180: discriminating_predictions:\n181:   - A positive output gap raises inflation with a lag; a shock that lowers the gap lowers inflation. A purely statistical benchmark cannot produce this conditional prediction.\n182:   - A persistent inflation shock raises the Selic more than one-for-one in the long run (Taylor principle), and the real rate rises.\n183:   - Inflation converges to the survey anchor, not mechanically to the target; the gap between Focus and target is itself informative.\n184:   - A fiscal or external shock that moves the gap and FX has a signable, decomposable effect on inflation.\n185: \n186: known_limitations:\n187:   - Survey expectations are treated as an anchor, not as model-consistent one-step-ahead forecasts.\n188:   - The HP filter is two-sided within the estimation sample; endpoint estimates are biased and revised.\n189:   - Equation-by-equation OLS ignores simultaneity between the policy rate, the gap, and inflation.\n190:   - The GDP block is a satellite bridge, not a structural IS curve, and is shared with the IBC-Br benchmark.\n191:   - The model is monthly and cannot capture intra-month Copom timing.\n192:   - The COVID dummy is a regime crutch; it is not a structural shock.\n193:   - Pseudo-vintages use release dates but not revision history.\n194: \n195: revision_policy:\n196:   max_rounds: 3\n197:   frozen_before_estimation: true\n198:   lockbox_visible: false\n199:   R1: baseline specification described above.\n200:   R2: replace the gap-implied IBC-Br path in the GDP satellite bridge with an AR(1) path on monthly IBC-Br growth. Targets the satellite mapping only; it cannot fix a structural refutation.\n201:   R3: replace lagged 12-month inflation in the Taylor rule with the Focus 12-month expectation (forward-looking rule). Keeps R2. Targets the Selic path only.\n202:   forbidden:\n203:     - adding new variables not declared in this card\n204:     - changing the estimation sample to improve scores\n205:     - using the lockbox for any decision\n206:     - more than one new hypothesis per round\n207:     - editing the card inside the loop\n208: \n209: pass_criteria:\n210:   blocking_high: [D1-refutation, D2, D3, D6, D7-sign]\n211:   core_target: ipca_12m\n212:   core_horizons: [6, 12]\n213:   core_rule: >\n214:     Theil U <= 1.05 vs random walk and MZ slope p >= 0.05 at h=6 and h=12,\n215:     and no significant loss to the Focus consensus (DM p >= 0.10 or\n216:     focus_ratio <= 1.05), and a documented value-added win over Focus at one\n217:     core horizon or a validation episode.\n218:   auxiliary_rule: >\n219:     Selic h=12: Theil U <= 1.05 and MZ p >= 0.05; otherwise\n220:     PASS_WITH_LIMITATIONS after the ladder. GDP is a satellite and is excluded\n221:     from the school's gate and mean rank.\n222:   medium_defects: >\n223:     D4 residual autocorrelation and a small output-gap magnitude are recorded\n224:     as limitations, not blockers, unless a refutation test fires.\n225:   terminal_states: [PASS, PASS_WITH_LIMITATIONS, REJECTED, UNESTIMABLE, UNRESOLVED]\n226: \n227: data_policy: >\n228:   Curated observations carry reference, release, and vintage dates. Pseudo-\n229:   vintages are built from release lags; revision history is not available for\n230:   most Brazilian series. Evaluation uses final-vintage outcomes only in the\n231:   evaluator. Validation targets are purged at 2019-12-31 so that no lockbox\n232:   outcome enters model selection. The lockbox 2020-01..2026-10 is sealed.\n233: \n234: provenance:\n235:   data_source: \"BCB SGS/Olinda, IBGE SIDRA\"\n236:   data_hash: \"recorded in registry manifest\"\n237:   code_hash: \"recorded in registry manifest\"\n238:   spec_hash: \"sha256 of this file\"","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /workspace/in/spec.yaml, lines 1-238\n1: school: new_keynesian\n2: model_id: new_keynesian\n3: version: 1.0.1\n4: status: frozen\n5: frozen_at: \"2026-11-23\"\n6: primary_target: ipca_12m\n7: validation_end: \"2019-12-31\"\n8: description: >\n9:   Monthly semi-structural New Keynesian QPM for Brazil. A hybrid NK Phillips\n10:   curve with survey expectations and an output gap, an IS curve driven by the\n11:   ex-ante real Selic rate, and a Taylor rule with an imposed neutral real rate.\n12:   GDP is a satellite bridge from IBC-Br; it is shared with the IBC-Br benchmark\n13:   and is not evidence for the New Keynesian block.\n14: \n15: changelog:\n16:   - version: 1.0.0\n17:     note: Initial pre-registration.\n18:   - version: 1.0.1\n19:     note: >\n20:       Data-contract and gate corrections before the second loop run. The\n21:       estimation start is now explicitly 2005-01 (the code previously began in\n22:       2003 because of the IBC-Br availability). The COVID dummy is used in\n23:       forecasts only when the origin is already inside the COVID window. The\n24:       gap description now states that the HP filter is two-sided within the\n25:       estimation sample, re-estimated at each origin, with no post-origin data.\n26:       GDP is marked as a shared satellite, not a school-specific forecast.\n27:       Refutation tests are machine-readable. Validation targets are purged at\n28:       the validation end so that no 2020 outcome enters model selection.\n29: \n30: equations:\n31:   is: >\n32:     gap_t = a0 + a1*gap_{t-1} + a2*gap_{t-2} + a3*(r_{t-1} - r*) + e_t,\n33:     where r_{t-1} = selic_{t-1} - Focus12m_{t-1} and r* = 4.0 is calibrated.\n34:   nkpc: >\n35:     pi_t = c + b1*pi_{t-1} + b2*pi_{t-2} + (1 - b1 - b2)*pi_e_t\n36:            + b4*gap_{t-1} + b5*dfx_{t-1} + e_t,\n37:     with long-run neutrality imposed (b1 + b2 + b3 = 1).\n38:   taylor: >\n39:     i_t - target_t - r* = rho*(i_{t-1} - target_t - r*)\n40:       + g1*(pi12_{t-1} - target_t) + g2*gap_{t-1} + e_t.\n41:     R3 replaces pi12_{t-1} with the Focus 12m expectation at t.\n42:   gdp_bridge: >\n43:     gdp_qq_t = c0 + c1*ibc_qq_t + c2*ibc_qq_{t-1} + e_t.\n44:     R1 constructs the IBC-Br path from trend growth plus gap changes.\n45:     R2/R3 construct it from an AR(1) on monthly IBC-Br growth. The R2/R3 path\n46:     is identical to the IBC-Br benchmark by construction; this is a satellite,\n47:     not evidence for the New Keynesian block.\n48:   expectations: >\n49:     pi_e_t is the monthly equivalent of the Focus 12-month-ahead median:\n50:     ((1 + focus12/100)^(1/12) - 1)*100. Survey expectations, not\n51:     model-consistent expectations.\n52:   gap: >\n53:     gap_t = 100*(log(IBC-Br_t) - HP_trend(log(IBC-Br_t))) with lambda = 129600.\n54:     The filter is two-sided within the estimation sample and is re-estimated at\n55:     each origin on data through the origin. No observation after the origin is\n56:     used. Endpoint estimates are biased and subject to revision.\n57: \n58: observables:\n59:   pi:\n60:     series: ipca_m\n61:     freq: M\n62:     transform: none\n63:     release_lag_days: 10\n64:   activity:\n65:     series: ibc_br\n66:     freq: M\n67:     transform: log, HP cycle\n68:     release_lag_days: 20\n69:   policy_rate:\n70:     series: selic_target\n71:     freq: D\n72:     transform: month-end last\n73:     release_lag_days: 0\n74:   expectations:\n75:     series: focus_ipca_12m\n76:     freq: D\n77:     transform: month-end last, monthly equivalent\n78:     release_lag_days: 0\n79:   fx:\n80:     series: fx_usdbrl\n81:     freq: M\n82:     transform: 100*dlog\n83:     release_lag_days: 1\n84:   gdp:\n85:     series: gdp_qq\n86:     freq: Q\n87:     transform: none\n88:     release_lag_days: 60\n89:     role: satellite\n90:     shared_with: ibc_bridge\n91:   inflation_target:\n92:     series: data/reference/inflation_targets.csv\n93:     freq: annual\n94:     transform: year-end target\n95: \n96: identification: >\n97:   IS curve: OLS with Newey-West (12 lags). The real-rate coefficient is\n98:   identified by variation in the ex-ante real Selic rate; r* is calibrated, not\n99:   estimated. NKPC: OLS with HAC; homogeneity is imposed, so the expectations\n100:   coefficient is 1 - b1 - b2 rather than freely estimated. Taylor rule: OLS\n101:   with HAC and the neutral real rate imposed, so the constant is not free.\n102:   The output gap is a filtered observable, not a latent state estimated by\n103:   Kalman filter. The model is estimated equation-by-equation; no system\n104:   estimator, no GMM, no IV. This is a forecasting QPM, not a full DSGE.\n105:   Simultaneity between the policy rate and inflation is a known limitation.\n106: \n107: calibration:\n108:   rstar: 4.0\n109:   hp_lambda: 129600\n110:   estimation_start: \"2005-01-01\"\n111:   covid_dummy: \"2020-03..2020-12; in estimation when identified; in forecasts only when the origin is inside the window\"\n112:   inflation_target_source: \"CMN annual targets; continuous 3.0% target from 2025\"\n113: \n114: estimation:\n115:   method: OLS with Newey-West HAC standard errors (12 lags)\n116:   frequency: monthly\n117:   start: \"2005-01\"\n118:   sample: expanding window\n119:   software: statsmodels 0.15\n120: \n121: forecast_recipe: >\n122:   At origin t (last business day of month M): IPCA and IBC-Br are available\n123:   through M-1; the Selic target and Focus 12m are available through t. The\n124:   Focus 12m expectation is held at its origin value in monthly-equivalent\n125:   terms. FX is a random walk after the first forecast month (dfx = 0). The\n126:   COVID dummy is set to 1 for forecast months inside 2020-03..2020-12 only when\n127:   the origin itself is inside that window; a pre-COVID origin cannot know the\n128:   regime, so the dummy is 0 for its forecast months. The policy rate path is\n129:   iterated from the Taylor rule; the gap and inflation paths are iterated from\n130:   the IS and NKPC. GDP is mapped by the shared satellite bridge. The 12-month\n131:   IPCA rate is derived by compounding the monthly path; it is never forecast\n132:   directly.\n133: \n134: refutation_conditions:\n135:   - A negative or statistically insignificant output-gap coefficient in the NKPC across sub-samples would refute the model's core transmission channel.\n136:   - A non-positive FX pass-through coefficient would refute the declared external channel.\n137:   - A non-negative real-rate coefficient in the IS curve would refute the demand channel.\n138:   - A non-positive inflation response in the Taylor rule, or a smoothing coefficient outside (0,1), would refute the policy block.\n139:   - Failure of Mincer-Zarnowitz calibration at the 12-month inflation horizon after the revision ladder would refute the model's forecasting claim.\n140:   - Failure to add value over the Focus consensus at the core horizons would refute the claim that the structural block is useful beyond the survey anchor.\n141: \n142: refutation_tests:\n143:   - id: nkpc_gap_significance\n144:     block: nkpc\n145:     key: gap_1\n146:     stat: tstat\n147:     op: abs_ge\n148:     threshold: 1.65\n149:     code: D1\n150:     severity: high\n151:     message: \"Output-gap coefficient in the NKPC is statistically insignificant; the card's core transmission refutation condition is triggered.\"\n152:   - id: fx_pass_through_sign\n153:     block: nkpc\n154:     key: dfx_1\n155:     stat: coef\n156:     op: gt\n157:     threshold: 0.0\n158:     code: D7\n159:     severity: high\n160:     message: \"FX pass-through coefficient is non-positive; the external channel violates the declared sign restriction.\"\n161:   - id: is_real_rate_sign\n162:     block: is\n163:     key: rr\n164:     stat: coef\n165:     op: lt\n166:     threshold: 0.0\n167:     code: D7\n168:     severity: high\n169:     message: \"Real-rate coefficient in the IS curve is non-negative; the demand channel is refuted.\"\n170:   - id: taylor_inflation_sign\n171:     block: taylor\n172:     key: pi_gap\n173:     stat: coef\n174:     op: gt\n175:     threshold: 0.0\n176:     code: D7\n177:     severity: high\n178:     message: \"Taylor-rule inflation response is non-positive; the policy block is refuted.\"\n179: \n180: discriminating_predictions:\n181:   - A positive output gap raises inflation with a lag; a shock that lowers the gap lowers inflation. A purely statistical benchmark cannot produce this conditional prediction.\n182:   - A persistent inflation shock raises the Selic more than one-for-one in the long run (Taylor principle), and the real rate rises.\n183:   - Inflation converges to the survey anchor, not mechanically to the target; the gap between Focus and target is itself informative.\n184:   - A fiscal or external shock that moves the gap and FX has a signable, decomposable effect on inflation.\n185: \n186: known_limitations:\n187:   - Survey expectations are treated as an anchor, not as model-consistent one-step-ahead forecasts.\n188:   - The HP filter is two-sided within the estimation sample; endpoint estimates are biased and revised.\n189:   - Equation-by-equation OLS ignores simultaneity between the policy rate, the gap, and inflation.\n190:   - The GDP block is a satellite bridge, not a structural IS curve, and is shared with the IBC-Br benchmark.\n191:   - The model is monthly and cannot capture intra-month Copom timing.\n192:   - The COVID dummy is a regime crutch; it is not a structural shock.\n193:   - Pseudo-vintages use release dates but not revision history.\n194: \n195: revision_policy:\n196:   max_rounds: 3\n197:   frozen_before_estimation: true\n198:   lockbox_visible: false\n199:   R1: baseline specification described above.\n200:   R2: replace the gap-implied IBC-Br path in the GDP satellite bridge with an AR(1) path on monthly IBC-Br growth. Targets the satellite mapping only; it cannot fix a structural refutation.\n201:   R3: replace lagged 12-month inflation in the Taylor rule with the Focus 12-month expectation (forward-looking rule). Keeps R2. Targets the Selic path only.\n202:   forbidden:\n203:     - adding new variables not declared in this card\n204:     - changing the estimation sample to improve scores\n205:     - using the lockbox for any decision\n206:     - more than one new hypothesis per round\n207:     - editing the card inside the loop\n208: \n209: pass_criteria:\n210:   blocking_high: [D1-refutation, D2, D3, D6, D7-sign]\n211:   core_target: ipca_12m\n212:   core_horizons: [6, 12]\n213:   core_rule: >\n214:     Theil U <= 1.05 vs random walk and MZ slope p >= 0.05 at h=6 and h=12,\n215:     and no significant loss to the Focus consensus (DM p >= 0.10 or\n216:     focus_ratio <= 1.05), and a documented value-added win over Focus at one\n217:     core horizon or a validation episode.\n218:   auxiliary_rule: >\n219:     Selic h=12: Theil U <= 1.05 and MZ p >= 0.05; otherwise\n220:     PASS_WITH_LIMITATIONS after the ladder. GDP is a satellite and is excluded\n221:     from the school's gate and mean rank.\n222:   medium_defects: >\n223:     D4 residual autocorrelation and a small output-gap magnitude are recorded\n224:     as limitations, not blockers, unless a refutation test fires.\n225:   terminal_states: [PASS, PASS_WITH_LIMITATIONS, REJECTED, UNESTIMABLE, UNRESOLVED]\n226: \n227: data_policy: >\n228:   Curated observations carry reference, release, and vintage dates. Pseudo-\n229:   vintages are built from release lags; revision history is not available for\n230:   most Brazilian series. Evaluation uses final-vintage outcomes only in the\n231:   evaluator. Validation targets are purged at 2019-12-31 so that no lockbox\n232:   outcome enters model selection. The lockbox 2020-01..2026-10 is sealed.\n233: \n234: provenance:\n235:   data_source: \"BCB SGS/Olinda, IBGE SIDRA\"\n236:   data_hash: \"recorded in registry manifest\"\n237:   code_hash: \"recorded in registry manifest\"\n238:   spec_hash: \"sha256 of this file\""}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/workspace/in/spec.yaml\"}"},"time":{"start":1795491115163,"end":1795491115437}}}}
